await write('overview', [head + ' — Big-picture orientation and mental model', '', '# Problem and purpose', '', 'Choosing a database is a workload and operations decision, not a popularity contest. First identify the data relationships, invariants, query shapes, consistency needs, team skills, recovery targets, and hosting constraints. Then compare the actual engine and service combination against those needs.', '', '# Plain-language mental model', '', 'A relational model stores facts in related tables and uses keys and constraints to protect relationships. A document model stores related fields in a document-shaped aggregate and can make that aggregate convenient to read or update together. Both still need schema rules, indexes, transaction boundaries, access controls, backups, and a plan for growth. “Flexible schema” does not mean “no schema design.”', '', '# How the parts fit together', '', '1. Describe entities, relationships, invariants, and which records must change atomically.', '2. List representative reads/writes, filters, joins/aggregation, data size, skew, and concurrent traffic.', '3. Choose candidate model and index/query shape that serve those access patterns while preserving integrity.', '4. Compare transaction, replication, backup, upgrade, and managed-service guarantees explicitly.', '5. Run a fair benchmark and restore/failover rehearsal using realistic data, durability settings, and failure cases.', '', '# Concept map', '', '| Decision | Relational model | Document model |', '| --- | --- | --- |', '| Data shape | Related tables and declared keys | Nested or referenced BSON documents |', '| Integrity | Constraints and transactions | Validators, atomic document updates, transactions where needed |', '| Query | SQL dialect and relational plan | MongoDB Query API and aggregation pipeline |', '| Scaling | Vertical, replicas, partitions, service tiers | Vertical, replicas, shards, service tiers |', '| Operations | Self-managed or provider service | Self-managed or managed/compatibility service |', '', 'These are design tendencies, not guarantees. SQL Server, PostgreSQL, and MySQL are all relational but expose different types, dialects, optimizer behavior, and deployment controls. MongoDB offers both single-document atomic updates and multi-document transactions; using a document model does not make every multi-record invariant atomic automatically. [SQL Server 2025](' + U.sqlServer + ') [PostgreSQL 18 docs](' + U.pgDocs + ') [MySQL manual](' + U.mysqlManual + ') [MongoDB data modeling](' + U.mongoModel + ')', '', '# End-to-end walkthrough', '', 'Suppose an ordering system has customers, orders, line items, and a reporting page. If the invariant requires a customer reference, unique order number, and a coordinated change across rows, a relational schema with primary/foreign keys and a relational transaction may fit naturally. If an order is usually read/written as a bounded aggregate, an embedded document may reduce read fan-out and give a single-document atomic update, but duplicated customer facts and growth bounds need a policy. In either design, test the reporting query, hot customer/order keys, concurrent writes, indexes, and recovery procedure. The model follows the workload and invariant, not the reverse.', '', '# When to use it and tradeoffs', '', 'Choose a relational design when relationships, cross-row constraints, and flexible joins are central. Choose embedded document aggregates when boundaries are clear, bounded, and commonly read/updated together. Referenced documents or normalized tables can avoid duplication and unbounded records but add fetch/join and consistency work. Managed services reduce some infrastructure work but add provider limits and service-specific upgrade/restore controls.', '', '# What later lessons cover', '', 'The first reference lesson maps data/query surfaces and indexes. The second compares transaction and consistency semantics. The third covers hosting, recovery, and ownership. The fourth teaches workload selection and benchmark method. SQL Server syntax is handled in t03-002 onward; provider-specific administration and pricing are out of scope.', '', '# Recent version changes', '', 'As checked on 2026-11-16, SQL Server 2025 is the current SQL Server line; PostgreSQL 18.7 is the current stable update while PostgreSQL 19 RC2 remains prerelease; MySQL 9.7.4 is LTS and 26.10 is Innovation; MongoDB 9.0.8 is the latest patch in its current stable major line. Treat the release channel and hosted availability as separate facts. [PostgreSQL release](' + U.pgNews + ') [MySQL October release](' + U.mysqlOct + ') [MongoDB release notes](' + U.mongoRelease + ')', '', '# Sources', '', bibliography].join('\n'));

await write('concepts', [head + ' — Core concepts and how they fit together', '', '# Learning objectives', '', '- Model the same requirement as tables or documents and name the tradeoffs.', '- Compare transaction and replication guarantees without relying on shared terminology alone.', '- Separate database engine choice from managed-service choice.', '- Design a representative test that can support a narrow, evidence-based conclusion.', '', '# Prerequisites', '', 'Know basic tables, keys, JSON objects, queries, transactions, indexes, replicas, and backups. This topic is a comparison framework; detailed SQL syntax and provider-specific administration are outside its scope.', '', '# Core concepts', '', '## Start with invariants and access patterns', '', 'Write down the business facts, uniqueness rules, references, deletion behavior, and which writes must be atomic. Then inventory query shapes and how often each runs. Relational tables make references/constraints explicit and support joins; document aggregates group data that is commonly accessed together. Embedding duplicates facts when they change independently; references can require more reads or a multi-document transaction. Either model can be poorly designed if it ignores actual access and update patterns.', '', 'A relational primary key identifies rows; foreign keys enforce reference rules. A document validator constrains writes but is not automatically equivalent to every relational constraint. Single-document atomicity in MongoDB is useful only when the invariant fits within that document. Across documents, MongoDB has multi-document transactions with topology and cost implications. [MongoDB atomicity](' + U.mongoAtomic + ') [transactions](' + U.mongoTx + ') [PostgreSQL constraints](' + U.pgConstraints + ')', '', '## Similar labels do not guarantee identical semantics', '', 'SQL Server, PostgreSQL, and MySQL implement relational transactions but differ in SQL dialect, data type/coercion, constraints, isolation, locking/MVCC, optimizer, and service feature exposure. Named isolation levels are not interchangeable proof of identical phenomena. MongoDB’s read concern and write concern name policies for read consistency and write acknowledgement; they should be matched to failure and freshness requirements. Synchronous replication only means a configured acknowledgement boundary; whether it waits for durable hardening, votes, or journal depends on the product/configuration. [SQL Server locking and row versioning](' + U.sqlTxn + ') [PostgreSQL isolation](' + U.pgTxn + ') [MySQL InnoDB transactions](' + U.mysqlInnoDb + ') [MongoDB read concern](' + U.mongoConsistency + ') [write concern](' + U.mongoWriteConcern + ')', '', '## Query and indexes are a coupled design', '', 'A query planner considers the query, statistics, index structures, and costs to select a plan. An index can reduce reads but adds storage and write maintenance. Compound-key order matters. Partial and expression index support/eligibility is vendor-specific. Full-text search and JSON-path indexing are specialized surfaces, not portable assumptions. Parameterized/prepared statements separate values from query text, yet plan reuse can be parameter-sensitive. Compare actual engine plans under realistic distributions instead of copying index syntax across vendors.', '', '## Scaling has multiple meanings', '', 'Vertical scaling adds resources to one deployment. Read replicas can add read capacity or recovery options but have lag/freshness considerations. Partitioning organizes data into pieces and can support pruning or maintenance; sharding distributes pieces across nodes and adds routing/rebalancing and cross-shard costs. These terms are related but not synonyms.', '', '## Managed service changes operational ownership', '', 'A hosted service can automate infrastructure, maintenance, backups, or failover, while limiting engine versions, extensions, privileges, or maintenance windows. A compatibility service such as Amazon DocumentDB is not identical to MongoDB simply because it supports some MongoDB APIs. Create a responsibility matrix for patching, key custody, recovery tests, networking, and incident response. Verify supported features for the specific service/version/region rather than inferring parity from the engine name.', '', '## Benchmarks must preserve workload shape', '', 'Record hardware/service tier, engine/version, schema, data size/distribution, query mix, indexes, concurrency, durability configuration, cache condition, and measurement window. Report successful throughput, p50/p95/p99, errors, and resource/cost use. A warm-cache single-query speed test does not predict cold-start, failover, write-heavy, or tail behavior. Keep benchmark conclusions limited to the tested setup.', '', '# Mechanisms and relationships', '', 'The decision sequence is: invariant -> data model -> query/index surface -> transaction/consistency contract -> deployment topology -> recovery ownership -> representative test. A mismatch discovered at any layer can change the candidate. Managed hosting can change operations without changing the conceptual data model; engine compatibility does not guarantee feature parity.', '', '# Worked examples', '', 'A tenant dashboard reads a tenant’s profile and recent orders, while order creation must be unique per tenant. Relational design can enforce `(tenant, order number)` uniqueness and a foreign key. An embedded order/customer snapshot may make a bounded dashboard read efficient but introduces update semantics for copied profile fields. Benchmark both with realistic tenant-size skew; test concurrent duplicate order creation, failover/read freshness, backup restore, and schema evolution. Choose based on the invariant and measured workload, not on one endpoint’s latency.', '', '# Misconceptions and edge cases', '', '- “NoSQL means no schema.” Documents still need shape, validation, versioning, indexes, and migration rules.', '- “ACID” means the same isolation behavior in every engine.', '- “A replica is a backup.” Replicas can copy deletes/corruption and may lag.', '- “Sharding is partitioning.” Sharding distributes across nodes; engine-local partitioning has a different failure and routing boundary.', '- “Managed means the provider handles recovery.” Customers still need to define and test recovery outcomes and validate their data.', '- “SQL-compatible means interchangeable.” Dialects, functions, types, constraints, and transaction behavior differ.', '- “An index always speeds the system up.” It can increase writes, storage, and maintenance.', '- “A benchmark ranks databases universally.” It only supports claims about its setup and workload.', '', '# Knowledge checks with answers', '', '1. What should drive embedding? **A bounded aggregate commonly accessed/updated together, with acceptable duplication and size.**', '2. Does MongoDB single-document atomicity automatically cover multiple documents? **No; use an appropriate transaction or model the invariant differently.**', '3. Is a read replica necessarily current? **No; verify the freshness/consistency policy and lag.**', '4. What must accompany a benchmark claim? **Workload, data, settings, versions, environment, metric definitions, and failure/cache conditions.**', '5. Does a managed engine guarantee feature parity with self-managed? **No; service limits and versions matter.**', '', '# Sources', '', bibliography].join('\n'));

for (const [groupId, items] of Object.entries(groups)) {
  const group = manifest.inventory.find(g => g.id === groupId);
  const compare = groupId === 'data-model-and-query-surface' ? 'Relational tables expose declared columns and relationships; BSON documents can nest an aggregate. SQL Server, PostgreSQL, and MySQL share relational foundations but differ in dialect and features. MongoDB’s query API and aggregation pipeline operate on documents. Index types and JSON support must be compared per engine/service, not by label alone.' : groupId === 'transactions-consistency-and-concurrency' ? 'Relational transactions commonly span statements/rows within a database; MongoDB single-document writes are atomic and its multi-document transactions expand that boundary with additional coordination. Read concern, isolation level, write concern, synchronous/asynchronous replication, and quorum are not synonyms. Compare the exact acknowledgement and read-freshness guarantees.' : groupId === 'availability-operations-and-service-models' ? 'Self-managed deployment offers control with operational burden. Managed services automate selected tasks but expose a supported subset and provider-specific responsibilities. Read replicas, HA replicas, partitioning, and sharding solve different capacity/availability problems. Backup/recovery evidence remains required across models.' : 'A decision method begins from workload and invariants. Latency, throughput, recovery, durability, TCO, expertise, and portability should be measured for the same representative conditions. A benchmark conclusion is a bounded report, not a database ranking.';
  const example = groupId === 'data-model-and-query-surface' ? '```text\nRequirement: order header, bounded line items, a unique order number per tenant, and reports by date.\nRelational candidate: normalized tables, tenant-scoped unique key, foreign keys, query planner and indexes.\nDocument candidate: embedded bounded order aggregate; validate document shape and index report paths.\nTest both query shapes and concurrent duplicate creation before choosing.\n```' : groupId === 'transactions-consistency-and-concurrency' ? '```text\nOne order document update: may fit MongoDB single-document atomicity.\nOrder + inventory in separate documents: define a multi-document transaction or redesign the invariant.\nIn any engine, test duplicate-key races, commit acknowledgement, replica lag, retry behavior, and durability configuration.\n```' : groupId === 'availability-operations-and-service-models' ? '```text\nCandidate A: self-managed engine; team owns patching, backup media, keys, restore, and failover.\nCandidate B: managed service; provider operates selected tasks, while team verifies feature/extension support, retention, restore, access, and cutover responsibilities.\nCompare the actual service matrix and rehearsed outcomes.\n```' : '```text\nBenchmark setup: same representative data and query mix, same durability target, stated concurrency, measured p50/p95/p99 and successful throughput.\nRun warm-cache, cold-start, write-heavy, and failover/recovery cases separately.\nReport tested versions, service tiers, index costs, and uncertainty; do not generalize beyond them.\n```';
  await write('reference-' + groupId, [head + ' — Reference: ' + group.title, '', '# Scope and version', '', 'Engine facts and release channels are pinned to the source snapshot checked 2026-11-16. Managed-service version/feature availability varies by provider and offering; verify the exact target. The list below contains only the supplied names.', '', '# How this group fits the mental model', '', groupId === 'data-model-and-query-surface' ? 'Model and query surface determine how facts are represented and how an application asks for them. The choice affects integrity, query expressiveness, indexes, and portability.' : groupId === 'transactions-consistency-and-concurrency' ? 'Transaction and replication terms describe different boundaries: what changes atomically, what concurrent work can see, and what the system acknowledges before replying.' : groupId === 'availability-operations-and-service-models' ? 'Deployment product and topology determine who operates which component and what happens during failure, scale-out, upgrade, and restore.' : 'Evaluation is the evidence layer that tests whether a candidate serves actual workload and business requirements.', '', '# Supplied names explained', '', items.map(bullet).join('\n\n'), '', '# Cross-item comparison', '', compare, '', '# Examples', '', example, '', 'These are decision sketches, not benchmark results or guarantees. Check the target engine/service documentation and test under the workload that matters.', '', '# Common mistakes', '', '- Inferring constraints or null behavior from one engine’s SQL syntax.', '- Embedding unbounded child data or duplicating facts without a consistency owner.', '- Comparing “synchronous” settings without knowing which acknowledgement is required.', '- Calling a compatibility service the original engine.', '- Counting a read replica as an independent backup.', '- Comparing only average latency or one warmed-up query.', '- Ignoring upgrade cadence, extensions, keys, restore time, and provider feature gaps.', '', '# Knowledge checks with answers', '', groupId === 'data-model-and-query-surface' ? '1. What tradeoff does embedding make? **Local aggregate access versus duplication, growth, and independent update needs.**\n2. Are JSON path indexes portable? **No; compare each implementation and query match rules.**\n3. Does a prepared statement guarantee an optimal plan? **No, parameter distribution and plan policy matter.**' : groupId === 'transactions-consistency-and-concurrency' ? '1. What does a read concern describe? **MongoDB read consistency/visibility policy.**\n2. Can a replica acknowledgment mean zero data loss? **Only under the exact configured commit/replication guarantee and failure assumptions.**\n3. What prevents a unique-key race? **A database-enforced unique constraint/index.**' : groupId === 'availability-operations-and-service-models' ? '1. Does a provider handle every restore responsibility? **No; define and verify customer/provider duties.**\n2. Sharding versus partitioning? **Sharding distributes across nodes; partitioning divides logical data, often within an engine.**\n3. What is RPO? **Maximum acceptable data-loss interval.**' : '1. Why measure p99? **To expose tail behavior hidden by means.**\n2. What makes a benchmark representative? **Realistic queries, data distribution, concurrency, durability, and cache conditions.**\n3. What limits the conclusion? **The tested versions, service setup, and workload.**', '', '# Sources', '', bibliography].join('\n'));
}

await write('practice', [head + ' — Exercises and worked solutions', '', '# Goals', '', 'Map requirements to data and transaction boundaries, expose product/service assumptions, and design a fair comparison.', '', '# Worked example', '', 'A reservation service needs one active reservation per room/time slot, a history view, and a tenant-specific dashboard. First state the uniqueness and overlap invariant and the required simultaneous update behavior. Compare a relational schema with declarative constraints/transactions against a document aggregate with a validator, single-document atomic operations, and any required multi-document transaction. Identify whether the invariant can live in one document and how a sharded key would route competing reservations. Then test realistic hot-room contention, duplicate races, p99, successful throughput, durability, backup restore, and failover. Do not infer a winner from model labels.', '', '# Exercises', '', '1. A customer profile contains a growing list of orders. Decide what evidence would favor embedding versus referencing or relational tables.', '2. Two services concurrently create the same tenant-scoped order number. What control prevents duplicates across service instances?', '3. An application reads from a replica after writing to the primary but must immediately see its own change. Which guarantee/configuration needs investigation?', '4. A team selects Amazon DocumentDB because it “is MongoDB.” What must be checked before migration?', '5. A benchmark reports 2x throughput but uses warm cache, no writes, and no durability. What conclusions are valid?', '6. A managed service supports the engine’s version but not a required extension. What are the decision options?', '7. The business requires 5-minute RPO and 20-minute RTO. What separate tests validate those targets?', '8. Current release labels show PostgreSQL 19 RC2 and MongoDB 9.0.8. Which should be called prerelease and which stable?', '', '# Progressive hints', '', '1. Check bounded document size, access/update patterns, independent child queries, change frequency, and duplication cost.', '2. Use a database uniqueness invariant at the correct tenant/key scope.', '3. Check replica read routing and read-your-writes/session consistency behavior.', '4. Check supported commands, operators, transactions, drivers, indexes, aggregation stages, and service gaps.', '5. Only a narrow warm-cache read-only result for that configuration.', '6. Change service/engine, redesign extension use, or accept migration/operational cost after a proof of concept.', '7. RPO tests recovery-point/data loss; RTO tests detection through validated service restoration.', '8. PostgreSQL 19 RC2 is prerelease; MongoDB 9.0.8 is a stable major-line patch as of the check.', '', '# Complete solutions', '', '1. Embedding is plausible only if the collection remains bounded and profile/order access/update patterns align. An unbounded growing array risks document growth and write contention; referencing or relational tables may fit separately queried/updated orders. Benchmark dashboard and order workflows and document ownership of copied customer fields.', '2. Enforce a unique key on the tenant identifier plus order number in the database. Application prechecks race because both services can observe “not present” before either write commits.', '3. Investigate whether the application can read from the same primary/session or use a documented read-your-writes/session guarantee. Replica lag and read concern are distinct from successful primary write acknowledgment.', '4. Verify API/command/aggregation/index support, transaction behavior, driver compatibility, version limits, consistency guarantees, and managed-service backup/restore/operations. Run application compatibility tests; compatibility is not identity.', '5. Only that setup’s warm-cache read-only throughput result. It says nothing about write-heavy performance, durability, cold start, p99, recovery, or a universal engine ranking.', '6. Compare other supported service/engine options, remove/rewrite the extension dependency, or accept self-management/porting cost. Validate the managed feature gap and operational consequences before deciding.', '7. For RPO, induce a failure and measure the latest recoverable committed data point from independent backups/replicas under stated settings. For RTO, time failure detection through restore/failover, validation, client reconnection, and useful operations. Rehearse both independently and together.', '8. PostgreSQL 19 RC2 remains a release candidate on 2026-11-16; PostgreSQL 18.7 is stable. MongoDB 9.0.8 is a stable patch in the MongoDB 9.0 line. MySQL 9.7.4 is LTS and 26.10 Innovation; those names identify separate channels. [PostgreSQL release state](' + U.pgNews + ') [MongoDB 9.0](' + U.mongoRelease + ') [MySQL release tracks](' + U.mysqlTracks + ')', '', '# Tests and edge cases', '', '- Include key skew, largest tenant, largest document, null/optional fields, and duplicate races.', '- Test transaction abort/retry and the exact replica read/write acknowledgement policy.', '- Measure index write/storage cost as well as query benefit.', '- Test managed-service limitations, driver compatibility, extension support, and maintenance windows.', '- Run backup restore and failover rehearsals against numeric RPO/RTO targets.', '- Separate cold start, warm cache, read-only, write-heavy, and failure workloads.', '- Record engine versions and patch/channel status at benchmark time.', '', '# Reflection questions', '', 'Which invariant must the database enforce? What is the atomic unit? Who owns migrations? What happens if the read replica lags? Which feature is unavailable in the hosted service? What evidence would change your choice?', '', '# Sources', '', bibliography].join('\n'));

await write('interview', [head + ' — Interview questions and reasoning', '', '# Core questions', '', '1. How do you select relational tables versus document aggregates?', '2. What does “schema flexibility” leave the team responsible for?', '3. How does single-document atomicity differ from cross-document transactions?', '4. Why can’t isolation-level names be compared without qualification?', '5. How do read/write concerns affect MongoDB operations?', '6. Partitioning versus sharding?', '7. Why are managed services not identical to engine installations?', '8. How do you compare a replica with a backup?', '9. How should a database benchmark be designed?', '10. What do current release channels mean for PostgreSQL, MySQL, and MongoDB?', '', '# Concise interview answers', '', '1. Start from invariants, aggregate boundaries, query/write access patterns, size/growth, and transaction scope; then test candidates.', '2. Validation, field evolution, indexes, data migrations, and consistency rules.', '3. A single-document write is atomic within that document; a multi-document invariant needs a transaction or different model, with coordination cost.', '4. Engines implement different concurrency mechanisms, snapshot scope, and conflict handling under similarly named levels.', '5. They define read visibility and write acknowledgement policies; choose them for stated consistency/failure assumptions.', '6. Partitioning divides data into manageable subsets; sharding distributes data across nodes and adds routing/rebalancing costs.', '7. Providers limit versions, extensions, commands, privileges, and maintenance/control surface while automating selected operations.', '8. Replicas improve availability/read scale but can carry mistakes and lag; independent backups support retained-point recovery.', '9. Use realistic data and query mix, concurrency, durability, cache state, versions, tail percentiles, throughput, failures, and full setup disclosure.', '10. PostgreSQL 18.7 is stable and 19 RC2 prerelease; MySQL 9.7.4 LTS and 26.10 Innovation are separate tracks; MongoDB 9.0.8 is a stable-line patch.', '', '# Deep follow-up answers', '', 'Discuss key/constraint semantics, parameter coercion and null behavior, index key order, snapshot/version retention, acknowledgement durability, replica freshness, provider-specific feature gaps, and upgrade/restore ownership. Be precise about what was tested. Avoid blanket claims that a document database needs no schema, that ACID implies serializable behavior, or that managed services remove customer responsibility.', '', '# Scenario questions', '', '**Two regions must accept writes during network partition.** Clarify whether conflicts are acceptable, how ownership/quorum works, and whether business invariants permit concurrent writes. State the consistency and reconciliation policy before proposing a topology.', '', '**A document design is fast until one customer grows to millions of child records.** Revisit aggregate bounds and hot-key/data-growth pattern; measure document size/update contention and consider referencing, partitioning, or sharding strategies supported by the target.', '', '**A managed service lacks a required extension.** Verify exact support boundaries and choose between redesign, different service, or self-managed engine with corresponding ownership cost.', '', '# Tradeoffs and alternatives', '', 'Relational normalization reduces duplication and supports declarative constraints but can require joins and index design. Document embedding can align atomic updates with aggregate access but risks growth/duplication; referencing adds lookups/transaction coordination. Managed hosting reduces some infrastructure work while limiting control/portability. Read replicas, HA replicas, partitions, and shards have different consistency and capacity roles.', '', '# Common weak answers', '', '- “SQL is always consistent; NoSQL is always eventual.”', '- “MongoDB is schema-free.”', '- “ACID means the same isolation in every product.”', '- “A read replica guarantees fresh reads.”', '- “Managed service means no operations.”', '- “A benchmark proves one database is faster.”', '- “A larger version number means newer stable release.”', '', '# Self-check rubric', '', 'Score 0–2: (0) preference or slogan; (1) names a product capability but misses its boundary; (2) starts from invariants/workload, distinguishes engine from service, states failure/consistency tradeoffs, and proposes a representative test.', '', '# Sources', '', bibliography].join('\n'));

console.log('Wrote t03-001 lessons for ' + allItems.length + ' exact inventory entries across 8 lessons.');
